@Tutorial(time: 20) {
   @Intro(title: "Debugging a Failure with Replay") {
      When Premise finds a failure it saves the minimised trace to disk and
      replays it on every subsequent run. This tutorial shows you what that
      looks like in practice and how to use replay to debug failures quickly.
   }

   @Section(title: "A Property That Fails") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         Start with a simple but buggy function: a cache that is supposed to
         be idempotent. Inserting a value twice should produce the same result
         as inserting it once. A bug in the implementation violates this.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Define the `BuggyCache` and write a property asserting idempotence.

            @Code(name: "ReplayTests.swift", file: "t10-s01-buggy-cache.swift")
         }
         @Step {
            Run the test. Premise finds a failure and reports the
            minimised counterexample. Note the failure output and the
            `.premise/examples/` path shown in the error.

            @Code(name: "Terminal", file: "t10-s02-first-failure.txt")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "How the Trace is Stored") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         After the first failure, Premise writes a JSON file into
         `.premise/examples/`. Current replay storage keys files by source
         file, `forAll` line, and strategy label. The logical
         ``PropertyIdentity`` can also carry the enclosing function name for
         metadata and equality; see <doc:FailurePersistenceAndReplay> for the
         distinction between logical identity and the storage lookup key.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Look at the generated trace file. It contains the minimised
            ``ChoiceTrace`` — the sequence of choices that reproduces the
            smallest failing input.

            @Code(name: "Terminal", file: "t10-s03-trace-file.txt")
         }
         @Step {
            Run the test a second time *without changing any code*. The engine
            loads the stored trace and replays it immediately — you see the
            same failure without waiting for random generation to rediscover it.

            @Code(name: "Terminal", file: "t10-s04-replay-output.txt")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "Using Replay to Debug") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         Because the replay is deterministic, you can add `print` statements,
         attach breakpoints in Xcode, or add extra assertions — and the exact
         same inputs will be exercised every time.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Add a `print` statement inside the property closure and run again.
            The same values are passed to the closure as in the first failure.

            @Code(name: "ReplayTests.swift", file: "t10-s05-debug-print.swift")
         }
         @Step {
            Fix the bug in `BuggyCache`. Run the tests again. The stored trace
            is replayed, passes, and fresh generation also passes. The stored
            trace remains — it is now a permanent regression case.

            @Code(name: "ReplayTests.swift", file: "t10-s06-fixed.swift")
         }
         @Step {
            (Optional) Clear the stored trace manually if you no longer want
            it replayed, or commit the `.premise/examples/` directory to
            ensure CI always verifies the regression.

            @Code(name: "Terminal", file: "t10-s07-clear-or-commit.txt")
         }
      }
   }

   @Section(title: "Replaying with a Fixed Seed") {
      @ContentAndMedia {
         An alternative to file-backed replay is fixing the random seed.
         A fixed seed produces the same sequence of inputs on every run.
         This is useful for quick reproducibility during development, but
         it does not persist across code changes that alter the strategy.
      }
      @Steps {
         @Step {
            Set `seed` in `PropertyConfig` to pin the random sequence.
            The property will run the same 100 inputs every time.

            @Code(name: "ReplayTests.swift", file: "t10-s08-fixed-seed.swift")
         }
      }
   }
}
